{"date": "2026-11-11", "post_id": 1509500, "title": "NGC 4449: Small Galaxy, Big Impact", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-11-ngc-4449-small-galaxy-big-impact/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Don\u2019t judge a galaxy by its size. Our home galaxy, the <a href=\"http://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/features/cosmic/milkyway_info.html\">Milky Way</a>, is about 100,000 <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/what-is-a-light-year/\">light years</a> across. This huge expanse of space contains hundreds of billions of stars! The smallest galaxies, called <a href=\"https://esahubble.org/wordbank/dwarf-galaxy/\">dwarf galaxies</a>, are about 1/10th the size of the Milky Way and only contain a few billion stars. They are also the <a href=\"https://www.eso.org/public/blog/little-galaxies-big-mysteries/\">most common type of galaxy</a> in the whole universe. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/dwarf-galaxy-ngc-4449/\">Today\u2019s image</a> shows one such dwarf galaxy, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2017-july-14-ngc-4449-close-up-of-a-small-galaxy/\">NGC 4449</a>, captured by the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/\">Hubble Space Telescope</a>. NGC 4449 and similar dwarf galaxies are <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/nasas-hubble-uncovers-case-of-missing-oxygen-in-nearby-dwarf-galaxies/\">missing more oxygen</a> than models predict. This suggests their <a href=\"https://esahubble.org/wordbank/stellar-wind/\">stellar winds</a> and <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/supernova/en/\">supernovae</a> are surprisingly effective at <a href=\"https://tenor.com/dLL7CwmYnWj.gif\">blowing away</a> oxygen and other heavy elements. Turns out these tiny galaxies may play a big role in spreading heavy elements \u2014 the building blocks of stars, planets, and life \u2014 throughout the universe!<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> a date in Poland", "credit": "NASA, ESA, A. Aloisi (STScI/ESA), and the Hubble Heritage (STScI/AURA)-ESA/Hubble Collaboration", "copyright": "NASA, ESA, A. Aloisi (STScI/ESA), and the Hubble Heritage (STScI/AURA)-ESA/Hubble Collaboration", "alt": "A large smear of stars, almost shaped like a bow-tie, covers most of the image. The center of the &quot;bow&quot; is full of pink clumps of gas, while the outsides consist of blue-white stars, streaks of dark dust, and more pink globs of gas.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-11-ngc-4449-small-galaxy-big-impact/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/hubble/galaxies/irregular/NGC%204449.jpg?w=2048&h=1320&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 11 - NGC 4449: Small Galaxy, Big Impact</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-11-ngc-4449-small-galaxy-big-impact/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Don\u2019t judge a galaxy by its size. Our home galaxy, the Milky Way, is about 100,000 light years across. This huge expanse of space contains hundreds of billions of stars! The smallest galaxies, called dwarf galaxies, are about 1/10th the size of the Milky Way and only contain a few billion stars. They are also the most common type of galaxy in the whole universe. Today\u2019s image shows one such dwarf galaxy, NGC 4449, captured by the Hubble Space Telescope. NGC 4449 and similar dwarf galaxies are missing more oxygen than models predict. This suggests their stellar winds and supernovae are surprisingly effective at blowing away oxygen and other heavy elements. Turns out these tiny galaxies may play a big role in spreading heavy elements \u2014 the building blocks of stars, planets, and life \u2014 throughout the universe!Tomorrow&#039;s picture: a date in Poland\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"ngc 4449: small galaxy, big impact\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"NGC 4449: Small Galaxy, Big Impact\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-11-ngc-4449-small-galaxy-big-impact/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/hubble/galaxies/irregular/NGC%204449.jpg?w=2048&#038;h=1320&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/hubble/galaxies/irregular/NGC%204449.jpg?w=2048&#038;h=1320&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A large smear of stars, almost shaped like a bow-tie, covers most of the image. The center of the &quot;bow&quot; is full of pink clumps of gas, while the outsides consist of blue-white stars, streaks of dark dust, and more pink globs of gas.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-11T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-10T13:54:50-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"NGC 4449: Small Galaxy, Big Impact\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/hubble/galaxies/irregular/NGC%204449.jpg?w=2048&#038;h=1320&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 11<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/hubble/galaxies/irregular/NGC%204449.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/missions/hubble/galaxies/irregular/NGC%204449.jpg\" alt=\"A large smear of stars, almost shaped like a bow-tie, covers most of the image. The center of the &quot;bow&quot; is full of pink clumps of gas, while the outsides consist of blue-white stars, streaks of dark dust, and more pink globs of gas.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> NGC 4449: Small Galaxy, Big Impact </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\nNASA, ESA, A. Aloisi (STScI/ESA), and the Hubble Heritage (STScI/AURA)-ESA/Hubble Collaboration\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Don\u2019t judge a galaxy by its size. Our home galaxy, the <a href=\"http://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/features/cosmic/milkyway_info.html\">Milky Way</a>, is about 100,000 <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/what-is-a-light-year/\">light years</a> across. This huge expanse of space contains hundreds of billions of stars! The smallest galaxies, called <a href=\"https://esahubble.org/wordbank/dwarf-galaxy/\">dwarf galaxies</a>, are about 1/10th the size of the Milky Way and only contain a few billion stars. They are also the <a href=\"https://www.eso.org/public/blog/little-galaxies-big-mysteries/\">most common type of galaxy</a> in the whole universe. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/dwarf-galaxy-ngc-4449/\">Today\u2019s image</a> shows one such dwarf galaxy, <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2017-july-14-ngc-4449-close-up-of-a-small-galaxy/\">NGC 4449</a>, captured by the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/\">Hubble Space Telescope</a>. NGC 4449 and similar dwarf galaxies are <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/nasas-hubble-uncovers-case-of-missing-oxygen-in-nearby-dwarf-galaxies/\">missing more oxygen</a> than models predict. This suggests their <a href=\"https://esahubble.org/wordbank/stellar-wind/\">stellar winds</a> and <a href=\"https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/supernova/en/\">supernovae</a> are surprisingly effective at <a href=\"https://tenor.com/dLL7CwmYnWj.gif\">blowing away</a> oxygen and other heavy elements. Turns out these tiny galaxies may play a big role in spreading heavy elements \u2014 the building blocks of stars, planets, and life \u2014 throughout the universe!<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> a date in Poland\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-10-saturn-behind-the-moon/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nKeighley Rockcliffe, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261111/html"}